+# Progressive triage policy and evidence selection
+
+## Scope in this change
+
+This change adds deterministic candidate grouping and evidence ranking, a versioned local policy store, and a settings page for the policy presets. It is the policy and selection foundation for the later Worker integration. It does not start an Agent, change an audit task, or enforce the displayed budgets yet.
+
+The modules live under `src/workbench/progressive_triage/`:
+
+- `contracts.py` defines canonical JSON serialization and the policy, grouping, and ranking versions.
+- `grouping.py` retains every observation and only groups exact duplicates with fixed source identity or findings with the same structured deterministic source-root-cause identity. Path, CWE, message text, and model prose alone never merge findings. Root-cause groups keep `root_cause_conclusion: not_inferred`; grouping is not a vulnerability confirmation.
+- `ranking.py` uses local source binding, observed security facts, unresolved evidence obligations, possible impact, source changes, and family coverage to select groups within a requested limit. Findings with incomplete source binding or unknown coverage are retained for manual review and excluded from automatic model selection.
+- `policy.py` validates fixed presets and custom values, and persists revisions in `progressive-triage-policy.json` under the configured workbench data directory. Writes use a process lock, an atomic replacement, a revision check, a restricted file mode, and a content digest.
+- `api.py` exposes `GET` and `PUT /api/v1/agent/triage-policy`. Updates reject extra fields and stale revisions. The existing local workbench request-header and same-origin middleware still protects writes.
+
+## Initial policy presets
+
+The economical, standard, and deep presets use the taskbook's initial suggested values. Standard starts at 30 quick-screen groups, 6 deep groups, 8 tool steps per deep group, 2 concurrent model requests, 1,800 seconds, and a suggested USD 25 budget. These values have not been calibrated through provider-billing or recall experiments. The USD amount is a policy suggestion, not a provider-enforced billing cap. Custom limits have strict integer bounds and cannot set the deep-group count above the quick-screen count.
+
+The settings page saves a policy revision locally. It explicitly reports that the Worker does not consume it yet. It also states that static-only audits remain at zero model calls and that source egress still needs the existing task-level preview and consent.
+
+## Selection and evidence boundary
+
+Selection means “eligible for a future bounded model review.” It does not mean safe, false positive, likely vulnerable, confirmed, or high severity. Every original observation remains in the reversible group manifest. Unselected candidates remain visible to manual review. The ranker never changes finding status or severity.
+
+Exact duplicate grouping and semantic root-cause grouping are reported separately. A semantic group records repeated exact-observation counts and preserves all member observations. When source identity is incomplete, candidates remain separate. Root cause remains `not_inferred` even when a deterministic identity is available.
+
+The grouping helper checks the fixed report/source binding and the shape of the deterministic identity; it does not independently re-read source or authenticate the producer's provenance string. Until a trusted Worker producer binds every referenced fact ID to source spans and exact source hashes, callers must omit semantic identities they cannot prove. The synthetic unit fixtures only verify grouping behavior and do not establish real-code root causes.
+
+## Four-level workflow and state model
+
+The intended path is:
+
+1. **L0, deterministic grouping:** source-bound exact duplicate observations are recognized; semantic grouping is allowed only for an identical deterministic entry/sink/mechanism/relation/source-fact identity. Every observation remains accessible in the group manifest.
+2. **L1, deterministic evidence ranking:** local evidence and family coverage sort groups. A configured quick-screen quota is a maximum for the whole audit operation, not a per-endpoint allowance.
+3. **L2, lightweight model screen:** a later phase may send only selected, source-bound groups after task-level source-egress consent. It should return structured dispositions with cited source ranges and evidence gaps; missing citations, parser uncertainty, or disagreement leaves the group unresolved.
+4. **L3, bounded deep Agent:** a later phase may investigate fewer groups using fixed source tools, explicit step/time limits, stop-on-no-progress, and recorded tool results. L2 or L3 model wording never changes a candidate to confirmed automatically.
+
+The proposed user-facing lifecycle should distinguish `unassessed`, `queued`, `in_progress`, `screened_unresolved`, `deep_investigation`, `stopped_budget`, `stopped_no_progress`, `blocked_source`, and human-reviewed outcomes. L2/L3 output must never set a confirmed disposition. A future human confirmation action must be added to the review contract explicitly; current review labels stay unchanged. This Phase 1 change stores none of those task outcomes and does not replace existing candidate states.
+
+## Policy and future operation schemas
+
+The current settings schema is `progressive-triage-v1`: `profile`, `limits`, `family_quotas`, mandatory `preserve_all_candidates`, and mandatory `consent_required`. The envelope has a monotonic local `revision`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, policy digest, and a bounded revision history. `PUT` requires `expected_revision`; stale writers receive HTTP 409, invalid or extra fields receive HTTP 422, and writes retain the existing same-origin workbench boundary.
+
+Future Worker integration should freeze the validated policy and digest into one immutable audit-operation record shared by single-revision, range, branch, and matrix endpoints. The operation ledger should track reservations and actual usage by phase, in-flight reservations, provider/model/price snapshot, and stop reason. Before each provider request, the shared ledger must atomically reserve the worst-case permitted usage; concurrent Workers may not spend the same remaining budget. Unknown provider completion or missing usage must settle conservatively or stop for review rather than be counted as zero. A service-side dollar value remains a soft estimate unless provider-side hard limits and an enforceable per-request upper bound are available.
+
+No database migration is needed in Phase 1: policy revisions live in a protected sidecar, and existing task/report/candidate schemas are untouched. A later task-snapshot migration must be additive, versioned, and able to read prior tasks without inventing policy state. Each report should include the frozen policy digest, grouping/ranking versions, selected and unselected group reasons, phase usage, and explicit unresolved/stop reasons.
+
+## Fail-closed conditions and follow-up plan
+
+Incomplete source binding, parser/coverage uncertainty, unsupported structured evidence, stale source identity, missing consent, static-only mode, budget reservation failure, provider usage ambiguity, or an exhausted time/call/token limit must stop or retain the candidate for manual review. The original static candidate set remains queryable and exportable. No plan may drop a candidate or label a non-selected candidate as safe.
+
+Phase 2 adds a consent-bound L2 request/response contract and local mock tests. Phase 3 adds immutable whole-operation snapshots, reservation/settlement ledger, concurrency and restart tests, and explicit stop handling. Phase 4 connects the shared ledger and selection policy to the single-revision and multi-endpoint operation paths without duplicating quotas. Phase 5 measures recall, cost, and user workflow on frozen vulnerable/fixed/safe holdouts and separately reports real-provider or browser evidence when available. The present change intentionally stops before those phases.
+
+## Integration conflict notes
+
+At the reviewed base, Draft PR #38 overlaps Workbench API registration and workspace view code; Draft PR #44 overlaps operations/settings UI paths. Issues #40 and #41 discuss operation-level and Agent-tooling integration. This change adds one route-registration hook in `src/workbench/api.py` and one policy tab in `WorkspaceViews.tsx`, while leaving operation routes, operation persistence, provider settings, task forms, Worker code, and Agent code alone. Before integrating with those changes, resolve route ownership and ensure their task creation paths can freeze one shared policy snapshot.
+
+## Integration boundary
+
+The current policy is not frozen into scan, revision-range, branch-comparison, or matrix task records. No Worker reads its settings, no global task budget is consumed, and no Agent call is triggered by the settings page. Existing consent, provider configuration, task review state, and disclosed-finding boundaries remain authoritative. Follow-up integration must first freeze one policy snapshot for the whole audit task, including all sub-endpoints, then make the Worker consume the same shared ledger; static-only must stay at zero model calls.
+
+The newest `main` base also has open Draft PR work in the Workbench API and settings UI. Review this change against those branches before integration; this implementation does not modify operation routes, provider settings, task creation forms, scanners, detectors, or Worker scheduling.
